        <section id="canary-deployment"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Canary deployment</h2>
              <p>Canary deployment (which is also known as ‘phased’ or ‘incremental rollout’) rolls out the latest version of a software product to a smaller group of users, so they can act as a test group to catch errors and issues before deploying to the wider group.</p>
<p>Canary deployment would alleviate capacity planning as the target audience is much smaller so the product would only need to be deployed to a single server or a limited set.  A subset of end users would test the update and alert the production team if there were any issues.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s1_p4_image.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>It is quite robust because if there are any issues with the new release, users can still be directed to the existing ‘live’ version whilst updates and bug fixes are being carried out on the Canary updated version.</p>
<p>One of the disadvantages is the resources that may be needed. This is because the time it would take to complete the updated version, as it is tested and phased into production, could be slow.</p>
<p>Also, version management would be a critical consideration as more than one version would need to be managed simultaneously.  However, rollback can be quick with this type of deployment if it was found that was needed.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
